Applied YAML but No Pod in Kubernetes? Let’s Fix It. 

You applied a Kubernetes YAML using kubectl apply -f pod.yaml...
It says "created" or "configured"... but kubectl get pods shows nothing?

🧠 What’s going on?
Kubernetes accepted your YAML — but didn’t actually create a Pod.

🔍 How to Troubleshoot:

1️⃣ kubectl get pods — Confirm the pod is actually there
2️⃣ kubectl get all — Maybe you created a Deployment or Service instead
3️⃣ Open your YAML — Check kind: (is it really a Pod?)
4️⃣ Check the spec: block — Is containers: present and correctly indented?
5️⃣ kubectl apply --dry-run=client -f pod.yaml — Validate structure before applying

🎯 Real-world example:
A YAML looked like a Pod… but it was actually a Deployment.
That’s why no pod showed up in kubectl get pods — it was controlled by a ReplicaSet!

📌 Tip: kubectl get all is your friend. It gives you a quick view of everything — Pods, Services, Deployments, and more.
